  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Areas of dense fog into the morning, and \npossibly again Monday night. Fog will impact travel conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nFog is the main concern. Areas of fog and low clouds are moving\nthrough the area, especially along the coast. The best chance \nof the dense fog is near the Downeast coast, but can't rule out \nsignificant fog further inland too. The fog should generally \ndissipate during the day Monday, but fog appears likely again \nMonday night. There may be a slightly later onset to the fog in \nthe evening Monday, but still think the fog could be locally \ndense by later Monday night. A Dense Fog Advisory is in effect \nuntil 9 AM Monday for Southern and Central Washington as well as \ncoastal Hancock counties.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Chance of showers through Monday afternoon, \nwith possibly some thunderstorms in Northern Maine Monday \nafternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION... \nAreas of drizzle moving from south to north along with the fog \nand low clouds overnight. Some drying expected into Monday \nmorning, but with a bit of clearing, expect enough instability \nfor showers and storms to fire off by about midday in north and \nwest areas. Scattered showers/storms are expected, with the best\nchance being inland away from the more stable marine layer. \nThat said, even in the wetter north, only going with a 40-60 \npercent chance of showers/storms, best chance in the western \nNorth Woods along the Quebec border. Monday will not be a \nrainout by any means. Cannot rule out a couple of the storms \nbeing on the stronger side with gusty winds, and there is some \nuncertainty how many storms can fire in the first place. Bangor \nand Downeast should remain dry, save for a shower or two. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Chance of showers and thunderstorms north of \nBangor Tuesday and mainly across northern Aroostook Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nThe axis of an upper level closed low that is gradually\nweakening as it passes to the north, then northeast Tuesday-\nWednesday, should bring with it sufficient instability in the\nlow-mid levels with its associated cold-pool aloft, that it\nshould produce scattered showers in areas north of Bangor on Tuesday. \n\nBy Tuesday afternoon, low to mid level lapse rates should be \naround 8C/km, with a few hundred J/kg of CAPE and around 35-45 \nkt of bulk shear. This should produce some strong storms across \nthe region north of Bangor. Wet bulb zero heights are around \n7500-8500 ft away from the coast so the greatest risk would be \nsmall hail.\n\nOn Wednesday, as the upper low weakens and retreats to the \nnortheast, the threat will be limited to the North, with low \nlevel lapse rates around 7.5-8 C/km, around 500-1000 J/kg of \nCAPE and around 20-30 kt of shear. Once again strong storms are \npossible with mainly isolated storms across northern Aroostook. \nThe wetbulb zero height is a little higher at 8500-9000 J/kg, \nbut still low enough to support hail. With the stronger CAPE, \nthe main threat is both strong winds and small hail in the \nstrongest convection across the North on Wednesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...Thunderstorms are possible Friday afternoon and\nevening, some of which may be on the locally strong side with \ngusty winds and small hail.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4 DESCRIPTION... \nA cold front crosses the area Friday afternoon and evening on \nthe leading edge of a full latitude trough pushing into the \nregion, with the added support of a northern stream shortwave \npushing through the trough around that time. With the potential \nfor 500-1500 J/kg of CAPE and 25-35KT of bulk shear, there is a\nrisk of some locally strong thunderstorms ahead of the front, \nwith the main threat strong gusty winds.",
